# Fallback security headers.
#
# `netlify.toml` is the source of truth. This file exists because a `_headers` file ships
# INSIDE the published bundle, so the policy survives a host that is configured from the
# dashboard rather than from the repository — and survives somebody moving the console to
# a different static host that reads `_headers` but not `netlify.toml`.
#
# If the two ever disagree, netlify.toml wins on Netlify. Keep them in step: TAB 13's
# acceptance is "fetch the deployed origin and read the response headers", precisely
# because a policy in a file is not a policy in production.

/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; connect-src 'self' https://api.<approved-domain>; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; upgrade-insecure-requests
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Permissions-Policy: accelerometer=(), camera=(), geolocation=(), gyroscope=(), magnetometer=(), microphone=(), payment=(), usb=()

/index.html
  Cache-Control: no-store, must-revalidate

# ── TAB 12 step 8: hashed assets are immutable ────────────────────────────────
#
# Every filename in these paths carries a content hash, so a changed file is a DIFFERENT
# URL. Caching them for a year is therefore safe by construction — and it is what makes
# `index.html: no-store` above affordable: the one uncached document is 4 kB, and
# everything expensive behind it is fetched once per deploy rather than once per visit.
#
# On a municipal connection that difference is the whole load time.
#
# `immutable` matters as much as `max-age`: without it a browser still revalidates on
# reload, which is a round trip per asset to be told nothing changed.

/*.js
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/*.css
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable

/media/*
  Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable
